WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.420
Prioritization methods are useful when they help people decide, not when they become another productivity project.

2
00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:10.520
The Eisenhower matrix separates urgent from important at a high level.

3
00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:17.260
Impact-versus-effort thinking helps identify work that creates meaningful value without excessive cost.

4
00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:22.160
Risk-based prioritization focuses attention where failure would hurt most.

5
00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:28.820
Dependency-based sequencing asks what must happen first so other work can proceed.

6
00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:31.500
Deadlines and service levels also matter.

7
00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:38.800
Some work has a fixed date, a contractual promise, an operational threshold, or a customer expectation.

8
00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:42.500
That does not mean every deadline should override everything else.

9
00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:48.600
It means deadlines should be evaluated with value, risk, dependency, and capacity.

10
00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:54.940
A deadline that cannot be met honestly should trigger scope negotiation rather than silent overcommitment.

11
00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:58.820
The idea of one most important thing can be powerful when used lightly.

12
00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:03.480
It asks: if today becomes messy, what must still move?

13
00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:08.780
That question helps protect the work that matters most from being buried under smaller demands.

14
00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:11.360
It does not mean only one thing matters forever.

15
00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:15.220
It means the current attention target is explicit enough to defend.

16
00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:19.580
Decision rules and escalation criteria reduce repeated debate.

17
00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:32.240
A team might decide that production incidents interrupt focus, routine messages wait for response windows, blocked dependencies escalate after a defined time, and new work requires a scope tradeoff when WIP is full.

18
00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:36.020
Lightweight rules are better than elaborate systems that nobody maintains.

19
00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:40.840
The best method is the one that creates clear decisions under real pressure.
